   Add 2 date columns that have values close to today
add_date_columns node1 =
    date4 = Date.today
    date6 = date4.date_add 8 ..Day
    node2 = date4.up_to date6
    table12 = node2.to_table
    column1 = table12.get 'Date'
    vector1 = Random.items column1 count=8
    table13 = table12.zip vector1
    table14 = table13.rename_columns [(Pair.Value 'Date' 'JoinDate'), (Pair.Value 'Value' 'PurchaseDate')]
    table15 = node1.zip table14
    table15

## ![One Component Example](/images/step-by-step-example.png)
   # Creating and Updating Columns with Formulas in Enso Analytics

   The set component in Enso Analytics is a powerful and flexible tool designed to enhance data manipulation by allowing users to add new columns or update existing ones in a table. This component provides a seamless way to enrich datasets with static values, dynamic expressions, or user-friendly, point-and-click simple expressions, catering to a wide range of analytical needs. By integrating with other Enso tools and leveraging temporal data enhancements, it supports robust data transformation workflows.

   ## Key Features

   - **Add or Update Columns**: The set component enables users to either create a new column in the table or modify the values of an existing column, offering versatility in data transformation workflows.
   - **Static Values**: Users can assign fixed values to a column, ideal for scenarios where consistent or predefined data points are needed, such as adding a data source identifier.
   - **Simple Expressions (Point-and-Click Builder)**: For users preferring an intuitive approach, the component includes a point-and-click expression builder. This feature simplifies creating expressions through a visual interface, making it accessible to users of varying technical expertise without manual coding.
   - **Expressions (Formulas)**: For advanced data manipulation, the set component supports custom expressions, allowing users to define complex calculations or transformations using Enso Analytics' formula syntax. More information on Enso's expression language can be found here: [https://help.enso.org/docs/using-enso/expression-syntax](https://help.enso.org/docs/using-enso/expression-syntax). This is perfect for deriving metrics or computed fields based on existing data.
   - **Flexible Integration**: The set component integrates seamlessly with other Enso Analytics tools, enabling column modifications as part of larger workflows, such as filtering, aggregating, or visualizing data.



   ## Use Cases

   - **Data Enrichment**: Add a new column like RecordSource with a static value (e.g., "Enso Sample Data") to categorize data origins.
   - **Dynamic Calculations**: Update or create columns with calculated values, such as computing Average Spend Per Visit by dividing Spend by Visits and rounding to the nearest whole number.
   - **User-Friendly Transformations**: Use the point-and-click expression builder to create simple formulas, such as classifying Region based on Latitude thresholds (e.g., >33.6 as "North", otherwise "South"), without coding.
   - **Text Case Adjustments**: Use native Proper([City]) for title case, Upper([City]) for uppercase, or Lower([City]) for lowercase to standardize text columns.
   - **Multi-Step Calculations**: Perform complex formulas in multiple steps, such as calculating a ratio and then rounding it, for better control over intermediate results.
   - **Multi-Column Formulas**: Apply sequential set operations to update multiple columns (e.g., RecordSource, Region, City) within a single workflow.


   ## Example Workflow

   In this example workflow, a table from data/customer_city_data.csv is processed using the set component, enhanced with date columns from the add_date_columns sub-flow, which adds dates near the current date when the workflow is run:


   - **Static Column Addition**: Use set to add a new RecordSource column with the static value "Enso Sample Data" for each row.
   - **Conditional Formula**: Use set with an IF/THEN/ELSE expression to create a Region column based on Latitude thresholds (>33.7 as "North", >33.6 as "Central", else "South").
   - **Title Case (Native 2025.3+)**: Use set with Proper(City) to create or update a City column in title case.
   - **Text Transformation**: Use set with an expression to create a Visits_text column by formatting Visits as a string with " Customer Visits" appended.
   - **Uppercase Modification (Native 2025.3+)**: Use set with Upper(City) to update the existing City column to uppercase.
   - **Multi-Column Update**: Use sequential set operations to add RecordSource and a simple Region (e.g., copying CustomerID), then update City to title case.
   - **Simple Expression**: Use set with the point-and-click ..Simple_Expr to create a Region column based on Latitude(>33.6 as "North", otherwise "South").
   - **Complex Single-Step Formula**: Use set with Round(Spend/Visits,0) to create an Average Spend Per Visitcolumn in one step.
   - **Complex Multi-Step Formula**: Use set in two steps: first calculate Spend/Visits into Average Spend Per Visit, then round it with a second set using round(Average Spend Per Visit,0).
   - **Legacy Workarounds (Pre-2025.3)**: For older versions, extract City, apply to_case ..Title or to_case ..Upper via mapping, and set the result to a new or existing column.

   ## Benefits

   The set component in Enso Analytics provides a flexible and efficient approach to creating and updating columns, supporting static values, complex expressions, native case functions (2025.3+), and user-friendly point-and-click formulas. Its ability to handle multi-step calculations and integrate with temporal enhancements via add_date_columns enriches dataset manipulation. The component’s seamless integration with Enso’s ecosystem, including filter, aggregate, or visualization tools (accessible via the eyeball icon), allows for immediate inspection and further processing. By combining ease of use with robust functionality, the set component empowers users to tailor their datasets for both simple and sophisticated analytical tasks in Enso Analytics.
main =
    node1 = Data.read 'data/customer_city_data.csv'
    ## Create a new Static Column - RecordSource is added with a value of Enso Sample Data for each row
    table2 = node1.set "Enso Sample Data" "RecordSource"
    ## IF/ELSE Conditional Formula. Adds a new Region column using the IF/THEN/ELSE expression.
    table9 = node1.set (expr 'if [Latitude] > 33.7 then \n"North" \nelse if \n[Latitude] > 33.6 then \n"Central" \nelse  "South"') "Region"
    ## New Modified Column - Title Case, Upper Case and Lower Case will be added to the expression language in 2025.3. Here is a workaround.
    column2 = node1.get 'City'
    text2 = _.to_case ..Title
    column3 = column2.map text2
    table3 = node1.set column3 "City_Title_Case"
    ## New Modified Column - New column created based on the Visits column
    table4 = node1.set (expr 'format([Visits]) + " Customer Visits"') "Visits_text"
    ## Modify Existing Column- Title Case, Upper Case and Lower Case will be added to the expression language in 2025.3. Here is a workaround.
    column1 = node1.get 'City'
    text1 = _.to_case ..Upper
    column4 = column1.map text1
    table1 = node1.set column4 "City"
    ## Formulas on multiple columns are done by simply adding additional set components
    table5 = node1.set "Enso Sample Data" "RecordSource"
    table7 = table5.set (expr 'if [Latitude] > 33.7 then \n"North" \nelse if \n[Latitude] > 33.6 then \n"Central" \nelse  "South"') "Region"
    ## Modify Existing Column- Title Case, Upper Case and Lower Case will be added to the expression language in 2025.3. Here is a workaround.
    column5 = table7.get 'City'
    text3 = _.to_case ..Title
    column6 = column5.map text3
    table6 = table7.set column6 "City"
    ## Use a Simple Expression which enables users to build expressions  using a point and click expression builder. 
    table12 = node1.set (..Simple_Expr (..Name 'Latitude') (..If (..Greater 33.6) "North" "South")) "Region"
    ## New Modified Column - Proper, Upper and Lower function added in 2025.3+
    any1 = node1.set (expr 'Proper([City])') "City"
    ## New Modified Column - Proper, Upper and Lower function added in 2025.3+
    any2 = node1.set (expr 'Upper([City])') "City"
    ## 2025.3+
    any3 = table7.set (expr 'Proper([City])') "City"
    ## Complex Formula Single Step. A new column Average Spend Per Visit is created in a single expression by dividing two existing columns and rounding the result to the nearest whole number
    table8 = node1.set (expr 'Round([Spend]/[Visits],0)') "Average Spend Per Visit"
    ## Complex Formula with multiple steps. This is the same calculation as above but in 2 steps instead of 1
    table10 = node1.set (expr '[Spend]/[Visits]') "Average Spend Per Visit"
    table11 = table10.set (expr 'round([Average Spend Per Visit],0)') "Average Spend Per Visit"



